  <language>en_US</language>
  <keyword>rmoutliers</keyword>
  <short_description>Detect and remove outliers from numeric data.</short_description>
  <syntax>
    <syntax_item>B = rmoutliers(A)</syntax_item>
    <syntax_item>B = rmoutliers(A, method)</syntax_item>
    <syntax_item>B = rmoutliers(A, 'percentiles', threshold)</syntax_item>
    <syntax_item>B = rmoutliers(A, movmethod, window)</syntax_item>
    <syntax_item>B = rmoutliers(..., dim)</syntax_item>
    <syntax_item>B = rmoutliers(..., Name, Value)</syntax_item>
    <syntax_item>[B, TFrm, TFoutlier, L, U, C] = rmoutliers(...)</syntax_item>
  </syntax>
  <description>
    <p><b>rmoutliers</b> detects outliers in a numeric vector or matrix and removes entries that contain detected outliers.</p>
    <p>For matrices, outliers are detected column-wise. By default rows containing outliers are removed. With <b>dim</b> equal to 2, columns containing outliers are removed.</p>
    <p>Detection methods and name-value arguments are shared with <b>isoutlier</b>. The <b>OutlierLocations</b> name-value argument can provide a logical mask directly.</p>
  </description>
  <examples>
    <example_item>
      <example_item_type>nelson</example_item_type>
      <example_item_description />
      <example_item_data runnable="cli"><![CDATA[A = [57 59 60 100 59 58 57 58 300 61 62 60 62 58 57];
B = rmoutliers(A)]]></example_item_data>
    </example_item>
    <example_item>
      <example_item_type>nelson</example_item_type>
      <example_item_description />
      <example_item_data runnable="cli"><![CDATA[A = [2 290 1 2; 1 0 323 1; 0 2 3 2; 1 1 2 3];
[B, TFrm, TFoutlier] = rmoutliers(A)]]></example_item_data>
    </example_item>
  </examples>
